## Summary

Refines the existing CI instead of replacing it with GitHub's generic Python package template.

## Changes

- keeps CI scoped to pushes to `main` and pull requests targeting `main`;
- separates Python-version compatibility from OS compatibility instead of running a full Cartesian matrix;
- tests supported Python 3.12 and 3.13 on Ubuntu;
- keeps Windows and macOS coverage on the canonical Python 3.12 runtime;
- keeps Ruff and strict mypy as blocking quality gates;
- keeps the full pytest suite on Linux, Windows, and macOS;
- moves wheel smoke into a dedicated packaging job instead of rebuilding the wheel for every OS/version combination;
- keeps CLI smoke coverage;
- upgrades pip before dependency installation;
- enables `setup-python` pip caching.

## Why

The previous workflow ran the same lint/type/test/wheel/CLI sequence on all three operating systems but only Python 3.12. That gave good platform coverage but no compatibility signal for another currently supported Python version and repeated packaging/lint work unnecessarily.

This layout makes the intent explicit:

- **quality**: Python compatibility + lint/type/test;
- **platform**: OS-sensitive behavior;
- **packaging**: wheel/install smoke.

The project declares Python `>=3.12`, so the 3.9/3.10/3.11 matrix from GitHub's generic template is intentionally not copied.

No Flake8 is added because Ruff is already the repository linting standard.

